course: dictionary
lesson: 5328
title: Words about everyday things
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A kettle is a container with a lid, a handle and a spout, and we use it for boiling water. Look at the clues in a sentence: «Put the kettle on, we need hot water for our tea.» The word kettle sits next to tea and hot water, so it must be something you use to heat water.
explain: Some words in a text describe a thing rather than name it. «The electric kettle switched off by itself.» Electric tells you the kettle uses power, and switched off tells you it stops when the water has boiled. Ask yourself what the sentence is telling you about the thing.
explain: Science words and safety words can use the same object. «Steam is invisible: the white cloud above a kettle is tiny droplets, and the real steam is in the clear gap just above the spout.» Here the kettle helps you learn that steam is a gas, and that the spout is where it comes out.
fact: A kettle is a container with a lid, a handle and a spout, used for boiling water.
fact: Phrases you may meet: put the kettle on, boil the kettle, electric kettle.
fact: An electric kettle switches off when the water boils, like the click you hear.
fact: In science, the steam from a kettle shows water changing from liquid to gas at 100 degrees Celsius.
fact: Kettles, steam and hot water can cause bad burns, so keep the cord away from the edge.
exercise: «She filled the kettle and pressed the switch.» What is a kettle? | A tool for cutting paper | A small wooden table | *A container with a lid, a handle and a spout, for boiling water
exercise: Mum says, «Boil the kettle for our tea.» What do you do with the kettle? | Wash the floor with it | *Boil water in it | Freeze ice in it
exercise: «Steam comes out of the spout of the kettle.» Which part of the kettle does the steam come out of? | The cord | The lid | *The spout | The handle
test: «Keep the cord away from the edge of the kettle.» Why do safety lessons say this? | To make the kettle boil faster | To make the kettle look neat | *To help stop hot water and steam from causing burns
test: «The electric kettle switched off when the water boiled.» What happened when the water boiled? | The kettle filled with ice | The kettle started to sing | *The kettle switched off
exam: «The white cloud above a kettle is tiny droplets, and the real steam is in the clear gap just above the spout.» Where is the real steam? | In the lid | *In the clear gap just above the spout | Inside the handle | In the white cloud
